Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for industrial I/O (read/write holding registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, isError, registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus responses require calling .isError() and indexing .registers[0]; pyModbusTCP/modbus-tk typically return None on error or raw 
    │   register lists directly, requiring rewritten error handling; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication, not native TCP, so using
    │   it here requires additional serial-to-TCP bridging or falling back to RTU wiring; unit/slave id parameter naming and defaults differ across libraries 
    │   (unit= vs slave_id= vs device address in constructor); connection lifecycle differs: pymodbus needs explicit .connect(), while some alternatives 
    │   auto-connect on first request; address offset conventions (0 vs 1 based, or including function-code offsets) can differ between libraries, causing 
    │   silent data misreads
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four libraries provide Python clients for the Modbus protocol (TCP or serial/RTU) capable of reading/writing holding registers, which is the sole 
        I/O operation performed in this file. pymodbus is the currently used TCP client with unit-based addressing and response objects exposing 
        .isError()/.registers. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk offer equivalent TCP master functionality but with different return types (raw lists/tuples instead of
        response objects) and different connection/unit-id conventions. minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution 
        target, though it is serial (RS-485/RTU) oriented and needs a TCP-serial bridge or different transport handling, plus a completely different read/write
        API and error model (raises exceptions rather than returning error-checkable objects).

